Q: Tint VectorDrawable inside Drawable Resource xml A: <p>You can define colors as attributes and reference them in your SVG. Then can load the drawable styled by a theme.</p>

<p>Short example:</p>

<p><strong>attributes.xml</strong></p>

<pre><code>&lt;declare-styleable name="vectorColors"&gt;
    &lt;attr name="primaryColor" format="color" /&gt;
    &lt;attr name="secondaryColor" format="color" /&gt;
&lt;/declare-styleable&gt;
</code></pre>

<p><strong>ic_icon.xml</strong></p>

<pre><code>&lt;path
    android:fillColor="?attr/primaryColor"
    android:pathData="...." /&gt;
&lt;path
    android:fillColor="?attr/secondaryColor"
    android:pathData="...." /&gt;
</code></pre>

<p><strong>styles.xml</strong></p>

<pre><code>&lt;style name="svgColors"&gt;
    &lt;item name="primaryColor"&gt;@color/someColor&lt;/item&gt; 
    &lt;!-- also can use #RRGGBB way --&gt;
    &lt;item name="secondaryColor"&gt;#ff0000&lt;/item&gt;
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Then load your theme and drawable:</p>

<pre><code>Resources.Theme theme = getResources().newTheme();
theme.applyStyle(R.style.svgColors, false);
VectorDrawableCompat drawable = VectorDrawableCompat.create(getResources(), R.drawable.ic_icon, theme);
imageView.setImageDrawable(drawable);
</code></pre>

<p>In your specific selector you should replace</p>

<pre><code>&lt;item&gt;
    &lt;bitmap
        android:src="@drawable/ic_icon"
        android:tint="@color/white"/&gt;
&lt;/item&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>by <code>&lt;item android:drawable="@drawable/ic_icon"&gt;</code></p>

<p><a href="https://medium.com/@emmaguy/dynamically-changing-svg-colours-on-android-b026a99137ad" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Reference and full example</a></p>

Q: How to set column width to autofit in Kendo Grid MVC A: <p>One way is to remove the "Scrollable" property or you can use this function in dataBound event.</p>

<pre><code>        function Autocolumnwidth(e) {
            var grid = e.sender;
            for (var i = 0; i &lt; grid.columns.length; i++) {
                 grid.autoFitColumn(i);
            }
        }
</code></pre>

Q: How to give a C++ thread a name, so that it I can identify it in a debugger? A: <p>In my tests, the value returned by <code>std::this_thread::get_id()</code> <em>does</em> match the value shown in the ID column of GDB's <code>info thread</code>.  You may just have to print it in hex to see that they agree, e.g. <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; std::this_thread::get_id()</code>, or store it in a variable <code>volatile auto stored_id = std::this_thread::get_id()</code> and then have GDB print it in hex <code>p/x stored_id</code>.</p>
<p>Alternatively, calling <code>gettid()</code> (from <code>&lt;unistd.h&gt;</code>) within a thread will return the number that's labeled <code>LWP</code> in GDB's <code>info thread</code> output.</p>
<p>Sample program:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;thread&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

void foo() {
    volatile auto stored_id = std::this_thread::get_id();
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;std::this_thread::get_id() = &quot;
              &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; std::this_thread::get_id()
              &lt;&lt; &quot;, gettid() = &quot; &lt;&lt; std::dec &lt;&lt; gettid()
              &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    while (true) { }
}

int main(void) {
    std::thread t1(foo), t2(foo);
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
